# Linux test matrix (WSL NAT)

Lived adhoc runs from this tree, as case rows. Not pytest.

	wsl.exe -d Ubuntu -u root -- bash /mnt/c/Users/derek/src/racoon2/samples/linux-matrix/run.sh
	./run.sh --cases 'unit|admin-empty'
	./run.sh --rebuild xfrm --prefix /usr/local/racoon2

`--rebuild` runs `./configure` only if there is no Makefile in `R2_SRC`
(default `/home/derek/src/racoon2`). It does **not** `systemctl enable
racoon2.target`. IKE rows with an empty `workers` cell use live
`racoon2-iked`. A numeric cell stops `racoon2-iked`, flushes leftover
SPD, restarts `racoon2-spmd` (IKE UDP bypass cache), flushes boot SPD,
runs `iked -F` with `RACOON2_CRYPTO_WORKERS`, restores systemd after
the case. `0` = inline; `1` = pool. IKE_SA DH is `crypto_job_submit`
(N=0 inlines).

Add a case: a row in `cases.tsv` + a `kind_*` in `kinds/<kind>.sh`.
Add a build: a row in `builds.tsv`.

Skip on purpose: `ikev1-strongswan` (msg5 vs 5.9.13). `lib/sample`.
Windows UDP into WSL NAT. Apple NAT-T / v6-in-v4.

RFC 9242/9370 rows live in `cases.tsv` as `gate=addke`: `i2ike-addke`
(intermediate), `i2ikesa-addke` (rekey), `i2iinit-addke` (init), plus the
drop/replay kill-tests `i2ike-drop` (R2 replay), `i2ike-drop576`
(R2 replay of a fragmented FOLLOWUP at MTU 576; box-green once, not in
the CI default — a container run held loss across the CREATE_CHILD
response and the new Y keymat never landed), `i2iinit-drop` and
`i2iinit-drop576` (H1 replay). The drop rows are counted-drop gates: PASS
requires the netem dropped counter (read from `tc -s qdisc show`, helper
`tc_dropped`) to agree with the replay marker — a marker with zero
measured loss, or loss without a marker, both fail.

New-row staging: `gate=box` rows (`i2ike-dup`, `i2ike-reqdrop`,
`i2iinit-charon`, `i2iinit-charonr`) run ONLY when
`R2_BOX=yes` (the box runner sets it; CI must not).  They stay out of
the Ubuntu/NetBSD full matrix and the Fedora container default until a
counted-gate PASS exists on the box AND a container run admits them —
same rule as `i2ike-drop576`.

RFC 6023 childless coverage (2026-09-29, all gate=box): the DAEMON now
implements the SA-less (modified) IKE_AUTH INITIATOR — `childless on`
on the iked initiator seat sends a SA-less IKE_AUTH (no SAi2/TSi/TSr)
and accepts the no-SAr2 response, the first ESP child landing via a
separate CREATE_CHILD_SA.  Seven coverage rows exercise both flavors,
SELF-CONSISTENT iked<->iked and charon/strongSwan INTEROP:
`i2iinit-childless-init` (initiator feature, PSK), `-init-rsa` (CERT+
push, review #2), `-rsa-charon` (CERT+ interop), `-init-cfg`
(CFG_REPLY push, review #2), `-cfg-charon` (CFG_REPLY interop via
charon vips), `-init-cfgneg` (FAILED_CP_REQUIRED refusal, review #2),
`-cfgneg-charon` (refusal interop).  Kind TRACE markers gate the actual
wire behavior: initiator `sending modified (SA-less) IKE_AUTH` /
`accepted SA-less response`, responder 16418 advertise + SA-less accept,
`pushing CERT+ (X509)`, `pushing [CP(CFG_REPLY)]`, and the
`childless peer message lacks required config payload` refusal.  The
`require_config_payload` knob (RFC 7296 2.19 / RFC 6023 s5) is newly
wired: it gates the childless AND childful ack of a mandatory
CFG_REQUEST, and is always OFF by default (no behavior change for rows
that do not opt in).  The `-cfgneg` rows are NEG PASSes: the refusal IS
the gate, ESP must NOT land, and the NDcPP compliance report demotes
the established/ESP-dependent cells to waived INFO.

Build manifest: the box runs `git archive HEAD`, scp'd as
`~/r2-matrix.tar.gz`; the archive hash is printed next to the iked
`staged/installed` hash proof every run, so the tree under test is
identified (latest green triple 2026-09-26 19:46 on `af87b6b`: the
aft archive sha256 = 52d3b2e6…).  Regenerate from the commit you intend
to test; never trust a repo-root `r2-matrix.tar.gz` (archives are
git-ignored, boxes may hold a stale copy).

Peer-backend swap: the i2i kinds default to iked<->iked, but a `-charon`
suffix on the case name runs the SAME kind with a strongSwan 6.0.7
(charon/swanctl, `ml` plugin) INITIATOR in the sibling initiator netns —
the responder stays the iked SUT.  A `-charonr` suffix is the reverse:
racoon2 iked INITIATES, charon takes the sibling RESPONDER seat (conn
loaded passively, no --initiate).  Shared peer helpers live in
`kinds/i2i_peer.sh` (swanctl conn write with ke1_mlkem768 + RFC 9242
IKE_INTERMEDIATE; spawn/load/trigger via charon+swanctl; gate evidence is
seat-dependent: initiator seat = charon `KE1_ML_KEM_768` selected +
`CONNECTING => ESTABLISHED`, responder seat = charon `selected proposal:
…KE1_ML_KEM_768` + `authentication of 'r2init-matrix' … successful`
(charon-as-responder does not emit the ESTABLISHED state transition —
it finishes via MGR checkin); cleanup).
This is the independent-implementation interop row: two ML-KEM-768
implementations derive the same SK(1), proven by the ESP child landing.
`R2_PEER_I=charon`/`R2_PEER_R=charon` force the seat globally for a batch.

NDcPP v3.0e compliance report: the i2i-family kinds (`i2ike`, `i2iinit`,
including the `-charon` / `-charonr` interop seats) end by running
`i2i_compliance` (`kinds/i2i_compliance.sh`), which emits one `CPL <cell>`
line per NDcPP FCS_IPSEC_EXT.1 A-cell (A1 SPD architecture, A2 default-
discard — counted via `/proc/net/xfrm_stat`, A3 tunnel mode, A4 ESP cipher,
A5 IKEv2/NAT-T socket, A6 IKE cipher, A7/A8 SA lifetimes, A11 DH group,
A12 IKE_SA>=CHILD_SA strength, A13 peer auth, A14 identifier binding) plus
a B2 key-establishment line; every line is read from the row's OWN retained
artifacts (netns xfrm policy/state, iked/charon logs, conf dir) — never
invented.  Unit-level cells (A9 DH-secret length, A10 nonce size, B1/B3-B6
crypto SFRs) emit INFO pointing at the KAT unit rows.

Standalone mappings for adjacent compliance scopes:
`doc/nsa-ipsec-hardening.md` (NSA briefs -> config),
`doc/usgv6-npd-compliance-matrix.md` (USGv6 NPD v1.3 device plan ->
racoon2 / platform / out-of-scope), and `mk_report.sh` Appendix A
(NIAP Functional Package for IPsec v1.0 element mapping, with the two
open FP items G1/G2).  A wired cell that
FAILs fails the case; INFO cells never do.  The kind calls it BEFORE netns
teardown (policy/SADB/counters must be live).

- `i2iinit-charon` (interop, gate=box): initial-IKE_SA ADDKE
  iked-responder vs charon-initiator on 192.0.5.x.  charon 6.0.7 places
  KE1_ML_KEM_768 in SAi1 (strongSwan 6.0 NEWS: RFC 9370 ADDKE transform
  types 1..7 + RFC 9242 IKE_INTERMEDIATE, ML-KEM via the `ml` plugin —
  NOT the vendored 5.9 that had no mlkem in the charon binary; prove the
  installed build with `swanctl --list-algs` needing a live charon, or
  `strings /usr/lib64/strongswan/libstrongswan.so.0 | grep mlkem`).  Box
  probe 2026-09-26 (id-fixed run): charon ESTABLISHED AES_GCM_16-256/
  PRF_HMAC_SHA2_256/ECP_256/KE1_ML_KEM_768, iked
  RES_IKE_AUTH_RCVD->ESTABLISHED + `IKE_INTERMEDIATE ADDKE round complete`
  + PRF_PLUS T1 recompute MATCH + keymat sha256=01a642e6.. g_ir_present=n,
  ESP in both netnss.

- `i2iinit-charonr` (interop reverse, gate=box): initial-IKE_SA ADDKE
  racoon2 iked INITIATOR (establish-sa, id r2init-matrix) vs charon 6.0.7
  RESPONDER seat on 192.0.5.x — the `-charonr` suffix loads the swanctl
  conn passively (no --initiate; local id racoon2-matrix, peer id
  r2init-matrix).  Same ML-KEM-768 SK(1) proof; the iked side must show
  `IKE_INTERMEDIATE ADDKE round complete` and charon (responder seat)
  must show `selected proposal: …KE1_ML_KEM_768` +
  `authentication of 'r2init-matrix' … successful`.

- `i2ike-dup` (review #5): CREATE_CHILD mid-handler duplicate unit.
  `netem duplicate 100%` on the initiator egress veth doubles the rekey
  CREATE_CHILD REQUEST back-to-back.  Dispatch (ikev2.c:538) must
  short-circuit the second copy BEFORE the handler — pre-arm as
  "dropping unordered message" (recv_message_id already advanced at
  ikev2.c:4429 before the GETSPI netlink yield), post-arm as "R2
  replay".  Gate: exactly ONE `CREATE_CHILD_SA request` handler entry
  (responder log, scoped to the rekey msgid) + rekey completes (new SPI
  both sides) + no abort + (marker lines for THAT msgid >= 1 OR the
  tcpdump `-X` msgid scan of the responder-ingress tap counts >= 2
  request copies).  The wire count substitutes because the box proved
  the pre-arm drop path is SILENT (2026-09-26: completed rekey, zero
  marker lines) — markers are only a proxy for "a duplicate landed";
  the tap count is the direct measurement.  (tshark display filters
  are dead on this Wireshark 4.6.8 build: isakmp.message_id and
  isakmp.exchange_type are rejected outright, so the count parses the
  msgid from the `-X` byte dump instead.)  Two handler entries = the
  window hole is real.  Deliberately classical ESP: the reassembler
  stores SKF fragments unconditionally (ikev2_frag.c:765) with a
  count-based completion gate (:778), so a duplicated mid-list fragment
  livelocks the assembly and an ADDKE FOLLOWUP row would fail for the
  wrong reason.  The DPD/NAT-D reply path has no yield between the msgid
  advance and the response arm, so it is unreachable by a wire
  retransmit (documented, no row).
- `i2ike-reqdrop` (review #4): REQUEST-direction loss.  Every preexisting
  loss row drops responder->initiator; this one nets the INITIATOR
  egress veth and drops the CREATE_CHILD REQUEST itself.  Recovery is
  the initiator's retransmit ladder (1,2,4,8...s), not a responder
  replay; no R2 replay is expected.  100% loss is held until the netem
  drop counter TICKS (that tick is the counted loss of the first
  request copy), kept ~4s so the 1s/3s ladder rungs are counted too,
  then the qdisc is REMOVED so the 7s rung arrives and FOLLOWUP +
  response complete inside the 10s ADDKE arm timer.  Gate:
  netem-dropped >= 1 on the REQUEST direction + the request logged
  strictly after qdisc removal + rekey completes + type-06 offer +
  matching ML-KEM keymat sha (g_ir_present=Y both sides) + no followup
  timeout.

RFC rows: `ikev2-netns-ikesa-rekey` (7296 §2.8), `ikev2-netns-cookie2`
(4555 COOKIE2/ADDITIONAL), `ikev2-netns-qcd` (6290 AUTH token),
`ikev2-netns-resume-dump` (SR2R dump after IKE_AUTH). Those IKE rows
stop live systemd iked (workers=0). Do not run them against a phone
session.

`ikev2-netns-childrekey` is charon-initiated PFS-19, no CP, no
racoon2 msgid mint. `ikev2-netns-r2rekey` is racoon2 as original
responder initiating CHILD rekey (RFC 7296 §2.2 msgid 0, charon
`rekey=no`, our ipsec lifetime 30s). Do not run IKE rows against
a phone session.
